From 7ac142fbb6e7bec062df659000ce5ea5de0ee6f7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vasilii Zolotukhin <vasilii.zolotukhin@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 13:01:22 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Mac: Plurium updates through Homebrew
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A minute after startup and then every six hours Plurium compares the
version in its published cask (Casks/plurium.rb in indapublic/plurium,
which is what `brew upgrade` installs) with the installed one, read from
the Homebrew Caskroom. A newer version shows an "Update" button at the
end of the unified tab strip and turns the "Check for Updates…" item
after "About Plurium" into "Update to <version>…".

Updating asks for confirmation, saves the session state like
chrome::AttemptRestart(), sets prefs::kWasRestarted and quits. A
detached script waits for the process to exit, runs `brew update` and
`brew upgrade --cask indapublic/plurium/plurium`, and reopens the app
with the same switches plus --restore-last-session, so every profile's
windows come back. The log is ~/Library/Logs/Plurium/update.log.

Copies not installed with Homebrew only link to the release page.
Automatic checks can be turned off with the PluriumAutomaticUpdateChecks
user default; --plurium-update-feed=<url> replaces the cask URL for
testing.
